==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
Alteration of {{m|en|-oma}}, removing the {{l|en|case ending}} retained from its {{etyl|grc|en}} etymon {{m|grc|-ωμα}}. Partially cognate to {{m|en|-some||body}}, from {{m|grc|σῶμα||body}}, in that both share the case ending {{m|grc|-μα}}, but the ω is unrelated.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# a [[mass]] of something

=====Derived terms=====
* [[biome]]
* [[environome]]
* [[genome]]
* [[phyllome]]

===Etymology 2===
{{back-form|mitome|lang=en|nodot=1}}, reinforced by {{m|en|chromosome}}. Early examples include {{m|en|biome}} (1916) and {{m|en|genome}}, from {{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|Genom}} (1920).<ref>{{R:OED2}}</ref> Some association with genetics due to occurrence in {{m|en|chromosome}} and {{m|en|genome}}.

====Suffix====
{{en-suffix}}

# {{context|biology|lang=en}} the complete whole of a class of substances for a species or an individual

=====Related terms=====
* [[-omics]]

=====Derived terms=====
{{suffixsee|en}}

===See also===
* [[Appendix:Suffixes -ome and -omics]]
* [[-some]]

